{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe parent-first cascade is implemented twice: `src/rebar/_commands/claim.py:200-237` (hardcodes\nthe `open` parent via `_resolve_open_parent`) and `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:421-493`\n(`_cascade_parent_first`, driven by the `_CASCADING_EDGES` table at `transition.py:414`). The\ntable was added after bug `cranial-sulfur-peafowl` and extended again for\n`paragonite-fruited-minnow` — both were \"an edge the cascade did not know about\" — and the claim\ncopy still cannot express any edge but `open→in_progress`. The ghost/init preconditions at\n`transition.py:308-323` and `claim.py:156-169` are byte-identical. Open task\n`convenient-unrational-songbird` covers the table's one-status-per-edge shape; this story is the\nsingle implementation it then builds on.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `src/rebar/_commands/lifecycle_cascade.py` with\n\n    cascade_parent_first(ticket_id, *, eligible_status, resolve_parent, advance,\n                         action, parent_action, cascade_seen=None) -> None\n\nextracting **the walk only**: the ancestor recursion, the cycle guard, the benign-race TOCTOU\nre-check, and the error rewrap that preserves `ConcurrencyMismatch` (and therefore exit 10) at\nthe leaf. `resolve_parent` and `advance` are injected, so each caller keeps its own WRITE\nPRIMITIVE — the only thing that genuinely differs between the sites.\n\n**The decision table is NOT moved.** `_CASCADING_EDGES` stays at `transition.py:415`, and\n`transition._cascade_parent_first` keeps its name, signature and `cascade` guard, delegating only\nthe walk. This is deliberate: the inbound writer from Gerrit 2044 ALREADY single-sources the\ndecision by importing `_CASCADING_EDGES` and `_resolve_parent_in_status`, so there is no second\ncopy of the decision to unify — only the walk is duplicated.\n\n`claim_compute` passes `eligible_status=\"open\"` with an `advance` closure recursing into\n`claim_compute`; `transition._cascade_parent_first` passes the edge's eligible status with an\n`advance` closure recursing into `transition_compute`. `action`/`parent_action` are two strings\nrather than one verb because no single word renders naturally in both message slots.\n\n`apply_inbound_events._cascade_inbound_status_parents` is deliberately NOT rewired: its walk\nreturns the written event paths topmost-first, an ordering its own suite pins, which a recursive\nhelper returning `None` cannot reproduce without complicating the signature for the two callers\nthat do not need it.\n\nGuard: `_CASCADING_EDGES` is defined exactly once under `src/rebar`\n(`grep -rnE \"^_CASCADING_EDGES\" src/rebar`).\n\n## Guard construction\nFollow the guard-test construction rule in the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner\nmodule exempt, match the constituent conjunction or AST shape named in Approach (or the\ndefinition count where no distinctive atom exists), `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape marker,\nand behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_commands/lifecycle_cascade.py` (new), `src/rebar/_commands/claim.py`,\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/_seam.py`,\n`tests/unit/commands/test_lifecycle_cascade.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nHappy path: claiming a child of an open parent claims the parent first on both surfaces. Edge: a\nparent already taken by another session raises `ConcurrencyMismatch` with the same exit code as\ntoday. Pinning: `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py` and the\ncascade tests from `cranial-sulfur-peafowl`/`paragonite-fruited-minnow` pass unchanged.\n\n## Code-reading corrections (summary; full detail in the ticket comments)\n1. The plan originally named `_cascade_open_parent`, which does not exist anywhere in `src/` or\n   `tests/`. The real helper is `transition._cascade_parent_first`. Corrected throughout.\n2. The cascade fires on THREE edges, not one: `(open→in_progress): open`, `(closed→open): closed`,\n   `(closed→in_progress): closed`. A consolidation built on the one-edge belief would silently\n   drop the reopen and reactivation cascades. `* → closed` must keep NOT cascading.\n3. `reopen` already cascades — it is a thin `transition(id, \"closed\", \"open\")`, so it inherits the\n   `(closed→open)` row. Bug `cranial-sulfur-peafowl` is therefore already fixed; the consolidation\n   must preserve that.\n4. There are THREE walks but only TWO decision copies, which is why the shared module extracts the\n   walk and not the table (see Approach).\n5. No test patches any cascade symbol by name and no string/`getattr` reference exists, so the\n   extraction is free to rename. The only couplings are lazy function-body imports and the\n   load-bearing `cascade=False` kwarg used by the replay seam at `_io/import_ndjson.py:347`.\n\n`despotic-prime-reindeer` (`bb73-97de-eeea-4899`) is `in_progress` with its work already merged as\nGerrit 2044, not an open bug as the plan first said.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `grep -rnE \"^_CASCADING_EDGES\" src/rebar` returns exactly one definition (`transition.py:415`); checked by the grep and the guard test. (The originally-written pattern `_CASCADING_EDGES\\s*=` could never match: the definition carries a type annotation between the name and the `=`, and `\\s` is not basic-regex anyway.)\n- [x] Every edge in the table is exercised by the same test for both `claim` and `transition`; checked by a parametrised test over the table.\n- [x] Pinning suites pass unchanged; checked by `pytest`.\n- [x] The inbound reconcile cascade that landed in Gerrit 2044 routes through the shared implementation, or the leaf records why it stays separate; checked by reading `apply_inbound_events.py` and the guard test.\n- [x] The leaf states whether `reopen` uses the consolidated cascade; checked by the description and, if it does, by a test.\n- [x] All THREE cascading edges keep working after the extraction; checked by the 32 tests in `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py` passing unchanged.\n- [x] The inbound cascade added by Gerrit 2044 keeps its own write primitive and its ordering guarantee; checked by `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_inbound_status_cascade.py` passing unchanged.\n- [x] `* -> closed` still does NOT cascade; checked by `test_close_does_not_cascade_to_parent`.\n- [x] The `cascade=False` replay suppression still works; checked by the two `*_cascade_false_suppresses_cascade` tests and `_io/import_ndjson.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787376210002593001,"uuid":"ad08b929-5645-4136-bf76-19bfe1359a8e"}